  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a disease vector’s climatically suitable range may expand under warming, both across latitude and up mountain slopes. On the left, a world map highlights the current suitable zone as a broad band centered on the equator, spanning much of the tropics between the Tropic of Cancer and Tropic of Capricorn. Narrower bands in cooler colors extend beyond this area into subtropical and temperate regions, indicating projected poleward expansion in both hemispheres.\n\nOn the right, the same idea is shown vertically on a mountain landscape. The current altitudinal range occupies lower elevations near sea level, while a larger, translucent band extends upward, representing projected upslope expansion as temperatures rise at higher altitudes. The text emphasizes increasing temperature in higher elevations, and the mountain profile suggests that areas previously too cool may become newly suitable.\n\nTaken together, the figure’s main message is that climate warming can enlarge the potential habitat of vectors in two directions at once: geographically toward the poles and locally toward higher elevations. This implies a broader future distribution than the present tropical, lowland-dominated range.",
